  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe key observation is that the optimal solution should have a smooth transition and minimal overlap with its shifts. Our approach is a hierarchical optimization that combines:\n- Multi-scale representation using base polynomials evaluated at harmonics\n- Shift-invariant optimization target using least-squares projection\n- Fast frequency-domain updates\n- Adaptive grid refinement to focus computation\n- Dictionary projection for smoothness enforcement\nThe central innovation is to represent the unknown function as:\n    h(x) = \u03a3_{k=0}^{K} a_k \u03c6_k(\u03c9 x)\nwith \u03c6_k being orthogonal harmonics, \u03c9 fixed frequency, and optimized coefficients a_k. This ensures shift-invariance through the formulation. We then solve a constrained least-squares problem on the coefficients.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables: \n    h = composite harmonic expansion on [0,2] with fixed frequency \u03c9\n    a_k: expansion coefficients in [0,1] ensuring h stays within bounds\nConstraints:\n    integral h(x) dx = 1\n    0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x by projecting back to feasible set\n\nObjective:\n    C_5 = max_{k=0}^{2n-2} |\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx| * dx\n\nFinite element projection of the optimization ensures the bounds are satisfied. We solve the equivalent constrained least-squares problem. The expansion coefficients are optimized while enforcing the frequency at each scale.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a) Check feasible parent from initial_h_values\n   b) Initialize parameters:\n        n0 = parent length\n        ref_steps = 80        # Reference to previous record\n        grid_factor = [0.7, 1.0]  # Grid sampling factors\n        \n   c) Phase 0: Coarse representation at n_coarse = 200\n      - Sample parent uniformly\n      - Set K=30 base harmonics\n      - Project to least-squares fit of given integral constraint and bounds\n      - Compute initial candidate h0 = inner expansion at higher resolution\n\n   d) Phase 1: Adaptive harmonic optimization\n      - Evaluate objective at coarse grid\n      - Use multi-frequency update if gradient improves\n      - Maintain trial frequency set F = {\u03c9: base*phase}\n      - Update: \u03c9_candidate from current \u03c9 + delta_freq\n      - Transition period: 150 from coarse to fine\n\n   e) Phase 2: Grid refinement with smoothness constraints\n      - Add adaptive grid points where gradients mount\n      - Update global best solution without breaking smoothness\n      - Stopping criteria: \u0394C_5 < 1e-4 over 20 samples\n\n   f) Validation and repair\n        Use constrained optimization:\n            minimize [obj(h)] with \n                constraints: integral=1, 0\u2264h\u22641\n            via Fourier-projection and gradient descent\n        Project h to feasible set using quadratic penalty\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   - Primary: Base spectrum shift function: periodicity-based\n   - Secondary: n_optimize \u2208 {40, 80, 160, 320} cluster points\n   - Tertiary: Automatic frequency tuning based on scale\n   - Adaptive grid update: when eigenvalue drops below threshold\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Phase 0: ~15s (n=200)\n   - Phase 1: ~350s (adaptive search with 32 threads)\n   - Phase 2: ~700s (grid refinement)\n   - Total: ~1100s\n   - Validate each candidate with repair operations\n   - Update schedule: every 1e4 iterations, prune low-frequency solutions\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Check np.allclose(sum(h)*dx, 1)\n   - Search for finite best_c5\n   - If grid becomes too large, revert to parent if score exceeds threshold\n   - Final return: (best_h, best_c5, len(best_h))",
